Q: Confusion on YUV NV21 conversion to RGB A: <p>Terms like &quot;most significant position&quot; are ambiguous, because it depends on the endian of the machine.</p>
<p>When all data types are 8 bits, there is an easy unambiguous specification: byte order. For example,</p>
<pre><code>unsigned char rgba[4];
</code></pre>
<p>would have the data stored as</p>
<pre><code>rgba[0] = r;
rgba[1] = g;
rgba[2] = b;
rgba[3] = a;
</code></pre>
<p>or <code>{r, g, b, a }</code> regardless of the endianness of the processor.</p>
<p>If instead you did</p>
<pre><code>int32 color = (r &lt;&lt; 24) | (g &lt;&lt; 16) | (b &lt;&lt; 8) | (a &lt;&lt; 0);
</code></pre>
<p>you would get</p>
<pre><code>{ r, g, b, a }
</code></pre>
<p>on a big-endian system, and</p>
<pre><code>{ a, r, g, b }
</code></pre>
<p>on a little-endian system.</p>
<p>Do you work on systems that have heterogeneous processors? Like maybe you have a CPU and a GPU? How do they know which endian the other is using? You are much better off defining the byte ordering.</p>

Q: Changing a stopword dictionary on Heroku&#39;s Postgres service? A: <p>I can't say whether you can get access to the Postgres environment on Heroku (given that it is fully managed, this is doubtful).</p>
<p>But, PG seems to ship with a <a href="https://github.com/postgres/postgres/tree/master/src/backend/snowball/stopwords" rel="nofollow noreferrer">selection of stopword dictionaries</a>. You can find out whether a stopword file of interest is available by attempting to create a dictionary using it.</p>
<pre><code>CREATE TEXT SEARCH DICTIONARY test_stopword_check ( 
  TEMPLATE = pg_catalog.simple, 
  STOPWORDS = french 
);

DROP TEXT SEARCH DICTIONARY test_stopword_check;
</code></pre>
<p>This succeeds on Heroku PG. Attempting this with a non-existent stopword dictionary should earn you an error instead, like:</p>
<p><code>ERROR:  could not open stop-word file &quot;/rdsdbbin_1/aurora-12.34.12.34.1.1234.0/share/postgresql/tsearch_data/klingon.stop&quot;: No such file or directory</code></p>
